# Vercel + Render Env Hygiene

When a task mentions Vercel, Render, or env vars, apply this checklist before and after changes.

## Vercel environment levels

- Always handle Vercel env vars with explicit scope awareness:
  - `development`
  - `preview`
  - `production`
- Treat these 3 levels as the minimum standard unless a task explicitly says otherwise.
- If a user asks to "set on Vercel", confirm or perform updates for all intended levels.

## Newline-safe writes

- Never use `echo "value" | vercel env add ...` in PowerShell (adds `\r\n`).
- Use newline-safe input instead, for example:
  - `cmd /c "echo|set /p=value" | vercel env add KEY production`
- Avoid wrapping env values in extra quotes unless the value intentionally contains quotes.

## Post-write verification

- After env updates, run `vercel env pull` for the target environment and verify:
  - no escaped `\r\n`
  - no leading/trailing whitespace
  - no unintended surrounding quotes
- If malformed values are found, replace them immediately.

## Render API safety

- Treat `PUT /v1/services/{id}/env-vars` as destructive: it replaces the full env set.
- Prefer additive operations for single vars.
- If `PUT` is used, send the complete intended env list and verify all keys afterward.

## OpenClaw URL sanity

- `OPENCLAW_GATEWAY_URL=http://127.0.0.1:18789` is development-only.
- Production must use the public HTTPS gateway URL (for example `https://openclaw-aida.onrender.com`).

## Communication requirements

- Always specify where each env belongs:
  - Vercel server env
  - Vercel `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` client env
  - Render service env
- Redact secrets in user-facing messages unless the user explicitly asks for full raw values.
